CAT = "ieqfaRegulators"; SEG = "REG"
Q = [
("Which body is responsible for both the prudential supervision and the consumer protection regulation of financial firms in Ireland?",
 "The Central Bank of Ireland", ["The Department of Finance", "The Pensions Authority", "The Competition and Consumer Protection Commission"],
 "The Central Bank of Ireland authorises and supervises regulated firms for both financial soundness and conduct. The CCPC informs consumers and enforces consumer law but does not supervise these firms."),
("Prudential regulation is mainly concerned with:",
 "Firms staying solvent and sound", ["How advisers word their adverts", "The ombudsman's fees", "How consumers file complaints"],
 "Prudential rules cover capital, liquidity and risk management so that a firm can meet its obligations. How firms treat customers is conduct, or consumer protection, regulation."),
("Consumer protection regulation is mainly concerned with:",
 "How firms treat their customers", ["An insurer's capital", "The stability of the euro area", "Which firms may merge with others"],
 "Conduct regulation, such as the Consumer Protection Code, sets standards for selling, advice, information, complaints and claims. Capital is prudential."),
("Systemic regulation looks mainly at:",
 "Risks to the whole financial system", ["The conduct of one adviser", "A single customer's complaint about service", "The wording of individual policy documents"],
 "Systemic, or macroprudential, regulation tries to prevent shocks spreading through the system, for example through limits on mortgage lending. Individual conduct is consumer protection."),
("The Central Bank's limits on loan-to-value and loan-to-income for new mortgages are an example of:",
 "Macroprudential regulation", ["Complaints handling", "Data protection regulation", "Anti-money laundering rules"],
 "The mortgage measures aim to keep household borrowing and bank lending sustainable across the system, which makes them macroprudential (systemic) tools."),
("Which body regulates occupational pension schemes and their trustees in Ireland?",
 "The Pensions Authority", ["The Revenue Commissioners", "The Data Protection Commission", "The Insurance Compensation Fund"],
 "The Pensions Authority supervises occupational schemes and PRSA compliance with the Pensions Act 1990. Insurers that provide pension contracts are also supervised by the Central Bank."),
("The Competition and Consumer Protection Commission's main role in personal finance is to:",
 "Inform and educate consumers", ["Authorise insurance brokers", "Decide disputes about claims", "Approve recognised qualifications"],
 "The CCPC publishes independent money information and comparison tools and enforces competition and general consumer law. It does not authorise insurance brokers or the other firms the Central Bank regulates."),
("Which institution directly supervises Ireland's largest, significant banks for prudential purposes?",
 "The ECB, with the Central Bank", ["The Central Bank acting alone", "The European Commission", "The Department of Finance acting alone"],
 "Under the Single Supervisory Mechanism, the European Central Bank directly supervises significant euro area banks, working through joint teams with the national supervisor."),
("The Central Bank's Administrative Sanctions Procedure lets it:",
 "Sanction breaches without a court case", ["Award compensation to each affected consumer", "Jail people found guilty of fraud", "Approve new financial products"],
 "The ASP is the Central Bank's own enforcement process for investigating and sanctioning breaches by regulated firms and individuals, such as fines, reprimands and disqualification."),
("After the Individual Accountability Framework, the Central Bank can take enforcement action against an individual who:",
 "Breaches their own conduct duties", ["Works for a firm that later made a loss", "Refuses to buy a firm's product", "Complains about a firm's service"],
 "The Central Bank (Individual Accountability Framework) Act 2023 lets the Central Bank pursue individuals directly for their own breaches, such as the Conduct Standards, not only for taking part in a firm's breach."),
("The Senior Executive Accountability Regime (SEAR) requires in-scope firms to:",
 "Map who is responsible for what", ["Pay staff a set minimum salary", "Publish every adviser's sales results", "Use only tied agents for sales"],
 "SEAR requires firms to document clearly where responsibility and decision-making sit within senior management, through statements of responsibilities and a management responsibilities map."),
("Under SEAR, the statutory Duty of Responsibility applies to:",
 "Holders of executive PCF roles", ["Every customer of the firm", "All part-time support staff", "The firm's external auditors and actuaries"],
 "Individuals in executive pre-approval controlled function roles in in-scope firms must take reasonable steps to avoid the firm breaching its obligations in their areas of responsibility."),
("The Common Conduct Standards apply to:",
 "Everyone in a controlled function", ["Only the board of directors", "Only claims staff", "Customers who buy a product"],
 "The Common Conduct Standards apply to all individuals in controlled function (CF) roles. Additional Conduct Standards apply to PCF holders and to roles that can significantly influence the firm."),
("Which is one of the Common Conduct Standards for individuals in controlled functions?",
 "Act in customers' best interests", ["Meet personal sales targets first", "Recommend the firm's products only", "Avoid any contact with the regulator"],
 "The Common Conduct Standards are to act with honesty and integrity, act with due skill, care and diligence, cooperate with the Central Bank, act in customers' best interests and treat them fairly and professionally, and observe proper standards of market conduct."),
("The Additional Conduct Standards under the Individual Accountability Framework apply to:",
 "PCF holders and CF-1 roles", ["All staff in customer service", "Consumers and their families", "Intermediaries in other states"],
 "The Additional Conduct Standards apply to individuals in pre-approval controlled functions and to CF-1 roles that can significantly influence the conduct of the firm's affairs."),
("Before dealing with a firm offering investments, a consumer can confirm that it is authorised by:",
 "Checking the Central Bank's registers", ["Asking the firm for its tax number", "Reading the firm's social media", "Contacting the Data Protection Commission"],
 "The Central Bank publishes registers of authorised firms and a warnings list of unauthorised firms. A clone firm may copy a real firm's name, so contact details should be checked against the register."),
("A clone firm is a fraudster that:",
 "Poses as a genuine authorised firm", ["Copies a competitor's prices", "Sells products under two names", "Merges with another regulated firm abroad"],
 "Clone firms use the name, address or registration details of a real authorised firm to win trust. Consumers should contact the firm only through details on the Central Bank register."),
("Under the Consumer Protection Code, the regulatory disclosure statement of a firm authorised in Ireland states that it:",
 "Is regulated by the Central Bank of Ireland", ["Is approved and guaranteed by the Department of Finance", "Is guaranteed by the Irish government", "Is endorsed by the Central Bank"],
 "The Code prescribes the wording: the firm's full legal name and trading names, then is regulated by the Central Bank of Ireland. The statement must not be presented as an endorsement."),
("A firm authorised in another EEA state that sells to Irish consumers must say it is authorised in its home state and regulated by the Central Bank of Ireland for:",
 "Consumer protection rules", ["Its capital requirements", "Its prudential rules", "Its auditing standards"],
 "A passporting firm stays prudentially supervised at home. In Ireland it follows the Central Bank's conduct of business rules, and its disclosure statement says so."),
("The Standards for Business Regulations 2025 were made under:",
 "Section 17A, Central Bank Reform Act 2010", ["The Consumer Credit Act 1995", "The Investor Compensation Act 1998 as amended", "The Pensions Act 1990"],
 "The Standards for Business are the Central Bank Reform Act 2010 (Section 17A) (Standards for Business) Regulations 2025. The detailed Code requirements are in the Section 48 Consumer Protection Regulations 2025."),
("The Investment Intermediaries Act 1995 mainly governs firms that:",
 "Receive and transmit investment orders", ["Underwrite general insurance risks", "Act as trustees of occupational pension schemes", "Lend money to buy homes"],
 "Investment intermediaries authorised under the 1995 Act receive and transmit orders in, and advise on, certain investment instruments, such as units in funds and tracker bonds."),
("The European Union (Insurance Distribution) Regulations 2018 transpose into Irish law:",
 "The Insurance Distribution Directive", ["The Mortgage Credit Directive", "The Payment Services Directive", "The Solvency II Directive"],
 "The 2018 Regulations implement the IDD, which sets registration, conduct, information and training standards for everyone who distributes insurance, including insurers' own staff."),
("MiFID II rules mainly apply to firms that provide:",
 "Investment services in financial instruments", ["Household and motor insurance", "Credit union savings accounts", "Mortgage arrears support only"],
 "The Markets in Financial Instruments Directive governs investment services such as advice, portfolio management and order execution in shares, bonds, funds and derivatives."),
("Why do governments regulate financial services more closely than most other markets?",
 "Consumers often know far less than firms", ["Financial products never lose value", "Firms ask the state to set all of their prices", "Most products are sold face to face"],
 "Financial products are complex and long term, and their quality often shows only years later. This information gap, and the harm when firms fail, is the main case for regulation."),
]
